I need to explain this in a clear, understandable way.The Bull and Bear Power Balance Algorithm
This algorithm, created by Vadim Gimelfarb and published in Stocks & Commodities Magazine (V. 21:10), attempts to quantify the actual strength of buyers (bulls) and sellers (bears) within each price bar by analyzing the internal price dynamics.
Core Concept
Instead of simply looking at whether a bar closed up or down, the algorithm examines where the real battle occurred within each bar's price range. It assigns portions of the bar's range to either bulls or bears based on specific price patterns.
How It Works
The algorithm evaluates three key factors:

Current bar's direction (close vs open)
Previous bar's direction (close[1] vs open)
Shadow dominance (upper shadow vs lower shadow)

The Logic Tree
Bull Power (BP) Calculation:
The algorithm asks a series of questions to determine how much of the bar's range represents bullish strength:

Is today a down bar? (close < open)

If previous was also down → Bulls defended from yesterday's close to today's low: max(high-close[1], close-low)
If previous was up → Bulls pushed from open to low: max(high-open, close-low)


Is today an up bar? (close > open)

If previous was also up → Bulls controlled entire range: high-low
If previous was down → Bulls either pushed from yesterday's close or controlled full range: max(open-close[1], high-low)


If close = open (doji-like), which shadow is bigger?

Evaluates based on shadow sizes and previous bar's direction



Bear Power (SP) Calculation:
Uses similar logic but inverted to measure bearish control:

Down bars show bear strength differently based on context
Up bars may still contain bearish resistance
Shadow analysis reveals where sellers appeared

Practical Example
Consider this scenario:
Previous bar: Bullish (close > open)
Current bar: Opens at $100, High $105, Low $98, Close $102

Bull Power measures: The push from $98 to $102 (bulls defended the low)
Bear Power measures: The rejection from $105 to $102 (bears resisted at highs)
Why This Matters
Traditional volume indicators just multiply volume by whether the bar was up or down. This algorithm recognizes that:

Even in a down bar, bulls may have fought hard (reducing bear power)
Even in an up bar, bears may have shown resistance (reducing bull power)
The previous bar's context affects interpretation of current price action

The Final Calculation
Total Power (TP) = Bull Power + Bear Power
Bull Volume Pressure = (BP/TP) × Volume  
Bear Volume Pressure = (SP/TP) × Volume
This gives a percentage-weighted volume showing what portion of the volume likely belonged to aggressive buyers vs sellers.
Key Innovation
Rather than a binary "all volume is bullish or bearish," Gimelfarb's algorithm distributes the volume proportionally based on the actual price battle that occurred within each bar, providing a more nuanced view of market dynamics.


